WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.861
A workflow is text with blanks in it.

2
00:00:04.861 --> 00:00:06.788
This step prints the branch name.

3
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:10.165
The part in dollar and double braces is an expression.

4
00:00:10.165 --> 00:00:14.501
And the text is filled in at two different moments, by different programs.

5
00:00:14.733 --> 00:00:16.160
Picture three places.

6
00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:17.315
GitHub's servers.

7
00:00:17.315 --> 00:00:20.033
The runner, the machine that runs a job.

8
00:00:20.033 --> 00:00:22.547
And the shell, bash, on that machine.

9
00:00:22.767 --> 00:00:23.491
Moment one.

10
00:00:23.491 --> 00:00:30.270
Just before the step starts, the runner works out each expression, and pastes the result into the text.

11
00:00:30.270 --> 00:00:32.771
On a push to main, the result is main.

12
00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:33.722
Moment two.

13
00:00:33.722 --> 00:00:36.872
The shell gets the finished script, and runs it.

14
00:00:36.872 --> 00:00:39.104
The expression never reaches bash.

15
00:00:39.104 --> 00:00:41.795
It was replaced before bash even started.

16
00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:43.983
Why does this matter?

17
00:00:43.983 --> 00:00:46.552
Say a step prints a pull request title.

18
00:00:46.552 --> 00:00:48.593
Someone else writes that title.

19
00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:51.151
The runner pastes the title in as text.

20
00:00:51.151 --> 00:00:53.080
Its quote mark ends your string.

21
00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:56.121
So bash runs a command the title added.

22
00:00:56.121 --> 00:00:57.857
Pasted text became code.

23
00:00:58.067 --> 00:01:02.788
The fix: put the value in env, and use dollar TITLE in the script.

24
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:05.381
Now the script itself never changes.

25
00:01:05.381 --> 00:01:09.680
The title travels as a variable, and the shell treats it as data.

26
00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:10.871
It just prints it.

27
00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:14.810
Now a job's own keys, such as if and runs-on.

28
00:01:14.810 --> 00:01:18.817
GitHub's servers work those out, before any runner exists.

29
00:01:19.033 --> 00:01:24.949
At that moment, GitHub only knows four contexts: github, needs, vars and inputs.

30
00:01:25.167 --> 00:01:29.246
The env, steps and runner contexts do not exist yet.

31
00:01:29.246 --> 00:01:32.540
So if env dot DEPLOY on a job is an error.

32
00:01:32.767 --> 00:01:36.891
When something behaves strangely, ask which of the two moments you are in.

33
00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:40.842
Now the other problem: passing a value along.

34
00:01:40.842 --> 00:01:42.871
Each step runs in a new shell.

35
00:01:42.871 --> 00:01:44.967
Each job runs on a new machine.

36
00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:49.151
So you append the value to a file the runner gives every step.

37
00:01:49.151 --> 00:01:51.827
Its path is in the variable GITHUB_OUTPUT.

38
00:01:52.033 --> 00:01:56.157
The step writes a line, version equals 1.4.0.

39
00:01:56.367 --> 00:02:01.461
When the step ends, the runner reads the file and records what you wrote.

40
00:02:01.461 --> 00:02:05.788
Later steps in this job read it as steps.meta.outputs.version.

41
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:10.138
Between jobs you need one more hop, because jobs share nothing.

42
00:02:10.138 --> 00:02:12.765
The job's outputs block names the value.

43
00:02:12.765 --> 00:02:15.983
When the job ends, the runner sends it to GitHub.

44
00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:19.917
GitHub hands it to every job that needs this one.

45
00:02:19.917 --> 00:02:23.787
That job reads it as needs.version.outputs.version.

46
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:25.661
Outputs are always strings.

47
00:02:25.661 --> 00:02:28.244
And never write a secret into these files.

48
00:02:28.244 --> 00:02:30.889
Everyone who can read the run can see them.

49
00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:36.186
Expressions are pasted in first, then the shell runs the finished text.

50
00:02:36.186 --> 00:02:41.499
And a value travels from a file, to a step output, to a job output, and through needs.
